                    round_name_r4:          `Defend & Win`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the drop-off, not the learners`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Resist the easy answer that the supervisors 'aren't motivated'. The <strong>same people complete at 82%</strong> in the live cohorts; the analytics show drop-off spiking <strong>after week 3</strong>, and they work rotating shifts across 9 sites with no protected time. This is a <strong>delivery-design failure</strong>, not a willpower failure: an 80% self-paced async build used the cheapest modality even for objectives that needed practice and interaction. Diagnose <em>where and why</em> learners disengage, and quantify the gap between the 31% trajectory and the 75% target — inside the BRL 2.4M ceiling.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Match each objective to the right modality`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Rebuild the modality map on <strong>fit, not cost</strong>. Knowledge and compliance transfer well asynchronously and stay cheap; <strong>skill rehearsal and behaviour change need synchronous or social formats</strong>. Then allocate the program hours across async (R$8/hr), live-online (R$35/hr) and in-person (R$120/hr). Watch both traps: leaving skill modules async because it is cheap is the original error — but swinging <em>everything</em> to in-person blows the R$2,4M ceiling and can't scale to 1,200 shift workers across 9 sites. The fix is the right blend, not the most expensive modality.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Sequence the blend & engineer engagement`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Turn the modality map into a sequenced journey and build the accountability the async-only design lacked. Sequence with the <strong>flipped model</strong> — async pre-work prepares learners for higher-value live practice. Then engineer the engagement mechanics: <strong>cohorts scheduled around rotating shifts</strong>, nudges, social commitment, manager involvement and protected learning time. The trap is real: a better modality with <em>no social glue</em> drops off the same way, and a mandatory live slot that clashes with shifts designs in another failure. Stay inside the ceiling by trading async scale against targeted synchronous touchpoints.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the blend & win the renewal`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Curveballs land. The client offers <strong>BRL 300k more — but only if completion hits 70%</strong> in the final four weeks; a key live facilitator becomes unavailable; and learners push back on shift-clashing sessions. Decide whether to take the conditional money and what it buys, solve the shift-clash <em>without abandoning synchronous learning</em>, and finalise <strong>cost per completing, competent learner</strong>. Then pitch the CHRO: she just watched a cheap program fail at 31%. She doesn't want cheap seats — she wants supervisors who finish and lead better. Reframe the BRL 9M renewal on cost per outcome.`,

