                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_design:        `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> This is a delivery-design failure — the cheapest modality was used even for objectives that needed practice, and the schedule ignored rotating shifts. Now you can fix the blend instead of running another motivational push that won't move the 31%.`,
                    coach_r1_motivation:    `<strong>Careful — 'low motivation' is the comfortable answer.</strong> The same supervisors complete at 82% in the live cohorts. People complete when the modality fits their reality. What in the <em>design</em> — not their willpower — caused this collapse?`,
                    coach_r1_content:       `<strong>Better content won't rescue the wrong modality.</strong> Polishing async modules that nobody finishes after week 3 misses the point: skill and behaviour objectives don't belong in solitary self-paced learning at all. Diagnose the modality fit first.`,
                    coach_r1_platform:      `<strong>It isn't mainly the platform.</strong> The LXP runs fine for the live cohorts that hit 82%. Blaming the technology dodges the real issue — objectives in the wrong modality and a schedule that ignores shifts.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as a delivery-design failure AND read the signal correctly: drop-off after week 3, 82% in live cohorts, no protected time on rotating shifts. That is exactly the evidence that justifies a redesigned blend, not a motivational campaign.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>The pattern is right there.</strong> Drop-off spikes after week 3, the live cohorts hit 82%, and the workforce is on rotating shifts with no protected time. 'Too noisy to diagnose' is how you end up repeating the same async design that failed.`,
                    coach_r1_lowhonesty:    `<strong>This is how the redesign fails before it starts.</strong> Low willingness to name the objective–modality mismatch means you'll tweak content and call it a fix. Own the design honestly or you'll repeat the 31%.`,
                    coach_r2_skilllive:     `<strong>Skill and behaviour belong in synchronous, social formats.</strong> Coaching, feedback and decision practice need peers and a facilitator — that is where the live cohorts' 82% came from. You've matched the objective to the modality on fit, not cost.`,
                    coach_r2_skillasync:    `<strong>This is the original error, repeated.</strong> Keeping skill rehearsal in solitary self-paced async because it is cheapest is exactly what drove completion to 31%. Practice and behaviour change need synchronous or social interaction — move them.`,
                    coach_r2_skillinperson: `<strong>Fully in-person maximises fit but not viability.</strong> In-person workshops everywhere give great practice, but at R$120/hour for 1,200 supervisors across 9 sites you'll blow the ceiling and can't scale. Live-online synchronous gets most of the fit at a fraction of the cost.`,
                    coach_r2_knowlive:      `<strong>You're spending live time on what could be async.</strong> Knowledge and compliance transfer well asynchronously and cheaply. Moving them to live wastes your most expensive, hardest-to-schedule hours on transmission you could have flipped.`,
                    coach_r2_knowinperson:  `<strong>In-person for knowledge transfer is the costliest mismatch.</strong> Policy and frameworks don't need a room and a facilitator — that is R$120/hour spent on content that async delivers for R$8. Keep knowledge async and save the expensive hours for practice.`,
                    coach_r2_overcorrect:   `<strong>You've over-corrected to the most expensive modality.</strong> Lifting completion by going heavy in-person blows past R$2,4M and can't scale to 1,200 shift workers across 9 sites — you've protected outcomes by destroying the margin. Async wasn't wrong for <em>everything</em>; which objectives genuinely still belong async?`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That's a genuine, viable blend.</strong> An async backbone for knowledge, meaningful live-online for skill and behaviour, and a modest in-person touch — high fit, inside the ceiling, scalable across 9 sites. This is Sagot's portfolio discipline, not a single favourite mode.`,
                    coach_r2_stillasync:    `<strong>Still async-heavy.</strong> Leaving the program mostly self-paced keeps it cheap but leaves the skill and behaviour objectives in the modality that failed. Shift more hours into synchronous practice where they actually belong.`,
                    coach_r3_flipped:       `<strong>The flipped model makes each modality do its best.</strong> Async pre-work carries the knowledge so the expensive live time is spent on practice and feedback, not lecturing. That sequencing is what lifts both effectiveness and completion.`,
                    coach_r3_livefirst:     `<strong>Live-first wastes your scarcest hours on transmission.</strong> Lecturing live then drilling async inverts the value: you spend R$35/hour on content async could have delivered, and the live time isn't used for the practice only synchronous formats enable.`,
                    coach_r3_parallel:      `<strong>Parallel tracks with no link is not a blend.</strong> Running async and live independently means neither prepares the other — learners get disconnected content and the same drop-off. Sequence them so async feeds live.`,
                    coach_r3_shiftfit:      `<strong>This fits the schedule to the learner.</strong> Multiple cohort times, regional facilitators and recorded-plus-live mean a supervisor on shift Tuesday afternoon can still join Wednesday morning. Synchronous learning for a shift workforce is an operational design, not just a pedagogical one.`,
                    coach_r3_fixedslot:     `<strong>One fixed mandatory slot designs in another failure.</strong> Tuesday 2pm clashes with a third of your supervisors' shifts across 9 sites — you've just recreated the completion problem. Shift workers need multiple cohort times or regional facilitators.`,
                    coach_r3_optional:      `<strong>All-optional live removes the accountability.</strong> Without cohorts, commitment and a schedule, optional sessions are skipped by exactly the busy, dispersed learners you most need to reach. Engagement design is what holds completion.`,
                    coach_r3_noengage:      `<strong>You've rebalanced the blend but added no social glue.</strong> No cohorts, nudges, manager check-ins or protected time means the new modality drops off like the old one. The original async failed precisely because it was solitary — where's the accountability?`,
                    coach_r3_noprotect:     `<strong>Synchronous sessions with no protected time produce resentment, not completion.</strong> Asking a shift supervisor to attend live with no paid, protected learning time recreates the drop-off. Negotiate protected time with the client as part of the redesign.`,
                    coach_r4_conditional:   `<strong>Smart way to take outcome-contingent money.</strong> The BRL 300k only pays if completion hits 70%, so committing it to a costed plan with a week-2 fallback trigger turns a gamble into a managed bet. You're betting on a target your redesign can credibly reach.`,
                    coach_r4_grab:          `<strong>You grabbed the BRL 300k with no plan to earn it.</strong> It only pays out at 70% completion and your trajectory started at 31%. What specifically in the redesign moves completion that far, that fast — and what's your fallback if week 2 shows it isn't working?`,
                    coach_r4_decline:       `<strong>Declining is safe but leaves outcomes on the table.</strong> The BRL 300k could fund the cohort and manager mechanics that lift completion past 70%. With a credible plan and a fallback, the conditional money is a managed bet, not a wild one.`,
                    coach_r4_hybrid:        `<strong>You solved the shift-clash without abandoning synchronous learning.</strong> Recorded-plus-live, regional facilitators and multiple cohort times keep the practice that drives effectiveness while fitting the rotating-shift reality — and it covers the lost facilitator.`,
                    coach_r4_cancel:        `<strong>Cancelling the live sessions throws away the fix.</strong> Reverting those hours to async puts skill and behaviour back in the modality that failed. The shift-clash is a scheduling problem — solve it with cohort times and recordings, not by abandoning synchronous learning.`,
                    coach_r4_force:         `<strong>Forcing the clashing slot and failing non-attendance is coercion, not completion.</strong> You'll get a paper number and resentment from a third of supervisors who physically couldn't attend. Fit the schedule to the learner instead.`,
                    coach_r4_outcome:       `<strong>That's the pitch that wins the renewal.</strong> The CHRO just watched a cheap program fail at 31%. Leading with cost per completing, competent learner — completion lift, effectiveness and a sustainable, scalable blend — answers what she actually buys, not the lowest seat price.`,
                    coach_r4_cheap:         `<strong>'We kept it cheap' is the wrong frame.</strong> She just watched a cheap program fail at 31% completion. She doesn't want cheap seats — she wants supervisors who finish and lead better. Reframe to cost per <em>outcome</em>: cost per completing, competent learner.`,
                    coach_r4_effort:        `<strong>Effort and 'lots of live sessions' isn't the case.</strong> The CHRO buys outcomes, not activity. Adding live for its own sake risks the overcorrection trap. Lead with completion lift, effectiveness and cost per completing learner — that is what justifies the BRL 9M.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
