                    insight_won:            `Renewal won: you turned a failing 31% async program into a credible ≥75% blend — skill and behaviour in synchronous formats, knowledge async, real accountability, inside the BRL 2.4M ceiling. You pitched cost per outcome, and the CHRO can justify the BRL 9M renewal.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You lifted completion but blew the BRL 2.4M ceiling — most likely by over-correcting to in-person. The outcome is real but the margin is gone and it won't scale to 1,200 supervisors across 9 sites. The fix was the right blend, not the most expensive modality.`,
                    insight_paper:          `Paper completion: the headline hit the target, but effectiveness lagged badly — forced attendance and coercion produced a number, not learning. This is the trajectory that collapses once the program ends.`,
                    insight_thin:           `You hit the number but learning is thin. Completion looks fine, yet without genuine objective–modality fit the supervisors didn't gain the skills — fragile, and a weak case for the renewal.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short of the ≥75% target. The account is now at risk and the BRL 9M renewal is unlikely. Re-examine which objectives you left in the wrong modality and where the engagement design was missing.`,
                    insight_engage:         `Engagement & accountability stayed low — you rebalanced modalities but never built the cohorts, nudges, manager involvement and protected time. A better modality with no social glue drops off the same way the solitary async did.`,
                    insight_loweffect:      `Learning effectiveness stayed low: objectives sat in the wrong modality. Skill rehearsal and behaviour change need synchronous or social formats — choosing modality by cost is the root error in the case.`,
                    insight_highcpo:        `Your cost per completing learner is high — the classic async trap: a 'cheap' program with low completion can cost more per outcome than a pricier blend at 80%. Measure cost per outcome, not cost per seat.`,
                    insight_lowcpo:         `Strong cost per outcome: by lifting completion with a fitted, engaged blend, your cost per completing, competent learner is excellent — exactly the metric that wins the renewal over a cheaper-per-seat competitor.`,

                    hint_overbudget:        `You're over the BRL 2.4M ceiling — likely too much in-person. Shift skill hours to live-online (R$35/hr vs R$120) and keep knowledge async; you get most of the fit at a fraction of the cost.`,
                    hint_paper:             `Completion is rising faster than real learning — you're building a paper number through coercion. Swap forced attendance for fitted modalities, flipped sequencing and genuine engagement mechanics.`,
                    hint_engage:            `Engagement is dangerously low. Fund cohorts, nudges, manager check-ins and protected time, and schedule live sessions around the rotating shifts — completion is a function of social and operational design.`,
                    hint_effect:            `Effectiveness is weak — objectives are in the wrong modality. Move skill and behaviour to synchronous/social formats and keep knowledge async; match each objective to fit, not cost.`,
                    hint_behind:            `You're behind the ≥75% target with little time left. Concentrate on the highest-leverage moves: skill→live, the flipped sequence, shift-fit cohorts and protected time.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — completion, effectiveness and engagement all healthy and inside budget. Now lock in the cost-per-outcome pitch to win the BRL 9M renewal.`,

